# Line endings, pinned. Rudra runs on Windows, macOS and Linux.
#
# Git's default on Windows is core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF
# on checkout. That is normally harmless, and here it was not: the vendored
# skill corpus is verified by hashing each file's RAW BYTES
# (src/rudra/skills/manifest.py), so a Windows clone changed all 55 files
# under src/rudra/skills/bundles/ and `verify_manifest` reported every one
# of them as `content changed:` -- reading a checkout artefact as tampering
# and rebuilding the rendered corpus under a different cache key. Measured:
# the same content as LF and as CRLF hashes to 0fc546b8… vs 8647ac91….
# See TODO.md CR-X2.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: detect text, normalise to LF in the repository,
# and check out LF on every platform regardless of core.autocrlf.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the corpus the manifest actually hashes. Stating it
# explicitly means a future `* text=auto` change cannot silently reintroduce
# CRLF here, which is the one place it breaks a correctness check rather
# than just producing a noisy diff.
src/rudra/skills/bundles/** text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF even on Windows: a CRLF shebang line makes the
# interpreter path unfindable (`/bin/sh\r: no such file or directory`).
*.sh text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the converse -- cmd.exe wants CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.whl binary
